kps.body_en:
All three traversals visit the left subtree before the right subtree; only the position of the node itself changes:

- **pre-order**: node → left → right;
- **in-order**: left → node → right;
- **post-order**: left → right → node.

Each runs in Θ(n) because every node is visited once. For a BST, the in-order traversal outputs the keys in increasing order — this is the BST property read recursively. Pre-order always starts with the root; post-order always ends with the root.

The predecessor of a key is the largest key smaller than it; the successor is the smallest key larger than it. For a node x:

- if x has a left subtree, its predecessor is the **maximum** of that left subtree;
- otherwise, the predecessor is the **lowest ancestor whose right subtree contains x** (walk up while x is a left child);
- the successor is symmetric: the minimum of the right subtree, or the lowest ancestor whose left subtree contains x.

Either way we follow one downward path and then one upward path, so the cost is O(h).

Many exam questions ask for an O(n) algorithm that annotates every node. The pattern is a post-order traversal: solve the left subtree, solve the right subtree, then combine at the node. Examples:

- **height**: `v.height = max(h(left), h(right)) + 1`, with an empty child having height −1;
- **subtree min/max**: the minimum is `v.left.smallest` if a left child exists, else `v.key`; the maximum is symmetric;
- **valid BST**: pass down an open interval (low, high) and require `low < v.key < high`, tightening it for each child;
- **balanced check**: return (height, balanced) and combine with `|hL − hR| ≤ 1`.

Each node is processed once with O(1) work after its children, so the total is Θ(n).

With 0-based indices:

- root: `A[0]`;
- children of `A[i]`: `A[2i+1]` and `A[2i+2]`;
- parent of `A[i]`: `A[⌊(i−1)/2⌋]`;
- the last non-leaf is `⌊n/2⌋ − 1`, so indices `⌊n/2⌋ … n−1` are leaves.

No pointers are needed; the shape is implicit in the indices. This is why Build-Heap can start at `⌊n/2⌋ − 1`: every later index is a trivial one-element heap.

Exam variants change the cost or the constraints:

- **cut cost c**: `v[i] = max(p[i], max_{1≤l≤i−1} (p[l] + v[i−l] − c))`;
- **at most Q pieces**: a 2-D table `dp[i][t]` over length and number of pieces, `dp[i][t] = max_l (dp[i−l][t−1] + p[l])`, with `dp[0][t] = 0`, `dp[i][0] = −∞`;
- **each cut loses one unit**: pieces have lengths y and i−y−1, so `v[i] = max(p[i], max_{1≤y≤i−1} (v[y] + v[i−y−1]))`.

Always re-derive the recurrence from what the cut produces; small wording changes flip the answer (the 2024 and 2025 papers both test this).

A table can be filled correctly iff every entry is computed after all entries it depends on. Read the recurrence and list the dependencies:

- `dp[i][j] = dp[i][j+1] + dp[i+1][j] + dp[i+1][j+1]` needs larger i and larger j first, so both loops must run downwards;
- LCS `S[i][j]` depends on i−1 and j−1, so both loops run upwards;
- interval DPs (matrix chain) need increasing interval length;
- knapsack 1-D needs the capacity loop downwards.

A loop order that violates a dependency silently reads stale (often zero) values and produces a wrong table.
